## Quick reference

| Method | What it returns | Typical use case |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|
| `.iter()` | `Iter<'_, T>` | Iterate by reference (borrow) |
| `.iter_mut()` | `IterMut<'_, T>` | Iterate mutable references |
| `.into_iter()` | `IntoIter<T>` | Consume the collection, move values |
| `.map(|f| …)` | `Map` | Transform each item |
| `.filter(|f| …)` | `Filter` | Keep only items that satisfy predicate |
| `.take(n)` | `Take` | First `n` items |
| `.skip(n)` | `Skip` | Skip first `n` items |
| `.fold(acc, |acc, x| …)` | `Fold` | Accumulate into a single value |
| `.collect()` | `Collect` | Gather into a collection (Vec, HashMap, etc.) |
| `.enumerate()` | `Enumerate` | Pair each item with its index |
| `.position(|x| …)` | `Option<usize>` | Find the index of the first matching item |

---

## TL;DR

1. **Iterators** are types that produce a sequence via `Iterator::next`.  
2. `for` loops are just sugar for `while let Some(x) = iter.next()`.  
3. Methods like `map`, `filter`, `take`, etc. return *new* iterators that are lazy and composable.  
4. You can chain them and finally call `collect()` to materialise the result.  
5. Any type implementing `Iterator` can be used in a `for` loop, and you can write your own by implementing the trait.

Happy iterating! 🚀
